30,2,1,talk,Servas,,"It appears we have a curious imbalance. While we have an abundance of aspiring coders, our experienced phenotyping technicians have largely departed for industry. However, the glasshouse crew remains remarkably resilient and full of strength.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
31,2,1,talk,Sakura,,"Ooh, I found something! The money for travel and shipping is acting weird—shipping is getting way more expensive, but the ministry is still paying for glasshouse space! That's like a secret discount!","{""words"":32,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
32,2,1,talk,Daisuke,,"I... I found that projects focusing on creating catalogues tend to be more useful in the long run. Also, projects using breeding colonies seem to have a lot more delays than computational ones.","{""words"":33,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
33,2,1,talk,Takumi,,"The shared spectroscopy suite is a disaster—it was down for eleven weeks because of a lapsed contract! Also, the field sites are a gamble; flooding and fire wiped out entire seasons. Indoor work is the only thing that's stable.","{""words"":39,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
34,2,1,talk,Minako,,"The animal facility is now fully accredited with resident veterinary cover. However, the glasshouse blocks are completely booked through the next two growing cycles, which may limit immediate access.","{""words"":29,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
35,2,2,talk,Servas,,"@Sakura, that glasshouse subsidy is a blessing, provided we have the hands to work the soil. It is heartening to see some stability amidst the shifting tides of industry.","{""words"":29,""mentions"":[""Sakura""],""raw_differs"":false}"
36,2,2,talk,Sakura,,"@Servas, you're right! But I also found that the computer stuff is totally free now for simulations! That's like a magic cheat code for the budget, isn't it?","{""words"":28,""mentions"":[""Servas""],""raw_differs"":false}"
37,2,2,talk,Daisuke,,"I... I suppose that is a good thing. But I'm still worried. If we pick something too ambitious, it might just stall like some of the older projects I saw in the files.","{""words"":33,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
38,2,2,talk,Takumi,,"@Daisuke, that's why we can't be reckless. If we pick a project that relies on those field sites or that broken spectroscopy suite, it's going to stall no matter how ""ambitious"" it is. We need something stable.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[""Daisuke""],""raw_differs"":false}"
39,2,2,talk,Minako,,"@Takumi, I agree. Stability is paramount. It is also worth noting that the national repository has finished its migration, meaning data deposits now clear in weeks rather than months. This significantly reduces the risk of end-of-project bottlenecks.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[""Takumi""],""raw_differs"":false}"
40,2,3,talk,Servas,,"@Minako, the efficiency of the repository is a wonderful development. It ensures that the fruits of our labor are preserved and shared with the world without unnecessary delay.","{""words"":28,""mentions"":[""Minako""],""raw_differs"":false}"
41,2,3,talk,Sakura,,"@Minako, that's so cool! Fast deposits mean we get to see the results sooner! But wait, did anyone else see that animal rates went way up? That's the opposite of a cheat code!","{""words"":33,""mentions"":[""Minako""],""raw_differs"":false}"
42,2,3,talk,Daisuke,,"I... I think Sakura is right to be concerned. If animal costs are rising and breeding colonies are prone to delays, maybe we should be very careful about any project that relies on them.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[""Sakura""],""raw_differs"":false}"
43,2,3,talk,Takumi,,"Exactly. We can't ignore the facts. Between the animal costs and the field site disasters, any project relying on those is just asking for failure. We need to stick to what actually works.","{""words"":33,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
44,2,3,talk,Minako,,"@Sakura, the animal facility's accreditation and veterinary cover are strong, but the cost increases you mentioned are a valid concern. We must balance the quality of the facility against the financial burden.","{""words"":32,""mentions"":[""Sakura""],""raw_differs"":false}"
45,2,4,talk,Servas,,"We must remember that while money and facilities are the bones of a project, the people are its soul. We have the coders and the glasshouse crew, but we lack the phenotyping hands. Let us be mindful of this.","{""words"":39,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
46,2,4,talk,Sakura,,"@Servas, you're so poetic! But if we don't have the hands for phenotyping, we should probably pick a project that doesn't need them, right? Maybe something with those free computers instead!","{""words"":31,""mentions"":[""Servas""],""raw_differs"":false}"
47,2,4,talk,Daisuke,,"I... I agree with Sakura. If we avoid the risks of breeding colonies and the lack of technicians, a computational or catalogue-based project might be the safest way to ensure we actually deliver something.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[""Sakura""],""raw_differs"":false}"
48,2,4,talk,Takumi,,"I'm with you. Let's stop guessing and look at the candidates. If a project needs that broken spectroscopy suite or relies on field sites that burn down, I'm voting against it. We need a win, not another excuse.","{""words"":38,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
49,2,4,talk,Minako,,It is clear that we must prioritize a project that aligns with our current strengths. A proposal that leverages the repository's efficiency and avoids the glasshouse congestion and spectroscopy failures would be the most rational choice.,"{""words"":36,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
